From: Datel OS
Subject: scan: a BSS heard far worse needs a clearly higher throughput estimate

wpa_scan_result_compar() sorts the scan results that the choice of a BSS
walks through. It caps both SNRs at GREAT_SNR (25 dB) and, when the capped
values are less than 7 dB apart, lets the estimated throughput decide. A
BSS at 19 dB therefore counts as close to one at 59 dB, and an estimate
that is 2 % higher puts it first.

Seen on a Creality K1 (BCM43430, 2.4 GHz, HT20) between two access points
of one network: an 802.11g one at -30 dBm (estimate 54000) and an 802.11n
one at -68 to -75 dBm (estimate 55250 at -70 dBm, 58500 at -69, 59800 at
-68). Whenever the far one was heard at -70 dBm or better it was chosen,
in 11 of 30 starts of wpa_supplicant. The link it gives is by far the
worse one, and without a background scan nothing moves the station
afterwards.

Let the estimate of a BSS that is at least 7 dB weaker, by the SNRs as
they are and not as capped, decide only if it is at least 20 % higher.
That is the margin wpa_supplicant_need_to_roam_within_ess() uses when the
current BSS has the better signal ("Skip roam - Current BSS has better
signal level"). A BSS that promises clearly more still wins. The cases
with a BSS in the 6 GHz band are left alone: there the comparison by
estimate is wanted whatever the SNRs are.

With the change the near access point stood first in the sorted results
of each of 54 starts on that printer, with the far one in the results
every time.

Upstream-Status: Pending

--- a/wpa_supplicant/scan.c
+++ b/wpa_supplicant/scan.c
@@ -2098,6 +2098,21 @@
 	if (snr_a && snr_b && (abs(snr_b - snr_a) < 7 ||
 			       is_6ghz_freq(wa->freq) ||
 			       is_6ghz_freq(wb->freq))) {
+		/* The SNRs compared above are capped at GREAT_SNR, so two BSSs
+		 * can count as close while one of them is heard far better.
+		 * The throughput estimate of the weaker one is the less
+		 * certain of the two: let it decide only if it is clearly
+		 * higher, by the margin that
+		 * wpa_supplicant_need_to_roam_within_ess() asks of a BSS with
+		 * a weaker signal. The 6 GHz cases are left as they are. */
+		if (!is_6ghz_freq(wa->freq) && !is_6ghz_freq(wb->freq)) {
+			if (snr_a_full - snr_b_full >= 7 &&
+			    wb->est_throughput < wa->est_throughput * 1.2)
+				return -1;
+			if (snr_b_full - snr_a_full >= 7 &&
+			    wa->est_throughput < wb->est_throughput * 1.2)
+				return 1;
+		}
 		if (wa->est_throughput != wb->est_throughput)
 			return (int) wb->est_throughput -
 				(int) wa->est_throughput;
